[{:seon.task/name "a-task-is-specified-once-a-proof-was-red"
  :seon.task/plan "A task's specification is its first proof (docs/plan.md Stage 1.2; the owner, 2026-11-14; root approved the written fact the same day). Evidence: 325 of 472 held backlog tasks with proofs have none written; random round 1's agent b6f1354dcc191912 spent turns 6-18 on a plan naming an uptime the page does not show and never wrote the proof its box scaffolded. Specified: one of the task's proofs has been red on its branch, it asserted what the code did not yet do (principles 3, a proof seen red first). History cannot answer it: a verdict keeps no history (:seon.test/status is :seon.db/no-history?, seon.facts.edn), so the fact is written once, when it is observed: the first status a turn reads (seon.turn) in which a proof of the task is red writes :seon.task/specified-turn, the turn's number, on the branch; an outside agent's at its hand-back's status. A branch cut from main does not hold it, so it is the branch's; it merges to main as any member of the task does (data's one three-way merge), and the ledger reads it as turns-to-specified (my.root/turns). seon.render.ui/status-hiccup opens its box list, while the task holds none, with: your task isn't specified yet; its first proof is the specification: write it where it goes, run (prove), and see it red for the reason the plan gives; if the plan does not match the code you read, fix the plan first, (swap! ctx assoc-in [:seon.agent/task :seon.task/plan] ...). The line goes once it is written, one named edit. No reason is added to the done rule and nothing gates the agent: specifying is a step, and the boxes stay the judge's reasons. Estimate: the turn's status is read already; one write a task, once. The render unchanged in order."
